       Estimating speed of the Grand Scheme of things

	One of the the most critical timing paths on the chip occurs 
during a Branch that tests the equality of a register to
the bus.  For example the microinstruction in PRIMITIVE-EQ

	((FROM *ARGS*) (BRANCH =BUS-VAL ...))

The path is:
	from PH1 
	Regcell driver for FROM-ARGS
	ARGS gated onto bus
	bus compared with VAL, driving the BUS=VAL line
	the condition selected by the BRANCH mux
	the OR of the BRANCH mux and the NEXT LSB.
	through the Micro PC switchyard
	through the MICRO PLA (*)
	through the NANO PLA (*)

	(*)  The Micro PLA inputs are the last part of the
	path that is gated by PH1, so some portion of the PLA
	delays can be overlapped with PH2.

	We will estimate each of the delay elements assuming
a process similar to NSIL II.

	The basic delay model is from Mead and Conway:

	Gate Delay = kt * Cl/Cg
	
	where k is the pullup/pulldown ratio (usually 4)
	      t is the transit time across the channel
		(.2 - .4 ns depending upon the process)
	      Cl is the load capacitance
	      Cg is the gate capacitance

	Some interesting parameters for the 2um lambda process are:
	
	Gate oxide capacitance		.5 fF/um**2
	Diffusion capacitance		.015 fF/um**2  with substrate bias
	Sidewall capacitance		.25 fF/um**2
	Field capacitance		.05 fF/um**2
	Poly sheet resistance		30 ohms/sq
	Diffusion sheet resistance	25 ohms/sq
	Metal sheet resistance		.05 ohms/sq

	Assuming a 2um lambda, Cg for a minimum size gate is

	16um**2 * .5fF/um**2  =	8 fF

Regcell driver
--------------

	The driver ANDs the FROM-ARG output of the NANO PLA
with PH1 and runs through a super buffer.
